  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Loring Rattray built timber and tourism businesses in South Africa. He took over his father's family interests and established Zululand's first commercial sawmill. He also owned the company that acquired MalaMala, where he ran the landholdings and safari business. His son Mike succeeded him in managing the reserve after his death.",
    "html": "<p>Loring Rattray built timber and tourism businesses in South Africa. He took over his father's family interests and established Zululand's first commercial sawmill. He also owned the company that acquired MalaMala, where he ran the landholdings and safari business. His son Mike succeeded him in managing the reserve after his death.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 51,
    "confidence": {
      "score": 81,
      "band": "medium",
      "reason": "Corporate history supports the regional sawmill claim; the shareholding account and court corroborate the separate tourism enterprise and succession."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S4",
      "S5"
    ]
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Timber businessman Loring Rattray sought new markets for the saligna grown in Zululand. Processing the wood commercially brought technical problems: wet timber could jam the saws. Jimmy Pringle, an experienced sawyer, advised him on sawing and curing and designed saws suited to the material.\n\nRattray also asked London's Imperial Institute to test whether saligna could supply the pulp used in rayon production. Positive results led him to seek overseas partners for a pulp mill near Kwambonambi. The textile company Courtaulds learned about the surplus timber through the institute and investigated the opportunity.",
    "html": "<p>Timber businessman Loring Rattray sought new markets for the saligna grown in Zululand. Processing the wood commercially brought technical problems: wet timber could jam the saws. Jimmy Pringle, an experienced sawyer, advised him on sawing and curing and designed saws suited to the material.</p>\r\n<p>Rattray also asked London's Imperial Institute to test whether saligna could supply the pulp used in rayon production. Positive results led him to seek overseas partners for a pulp mill near Kwambonambi. The textile company Courtaulds learned about the surplus timber through the institute and investigated the opportunity.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 92,
    "confidence": {
      "score": 82,
      "band": "medium",
      "reason": "Two company histories support the processing problem, named technical assistance and institutional testing; each contributor retains credit for their own work."
    },
